\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TObjReflInsertion1
\item[* relevant examples:] They perjured themselves
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_
ReflexiveSpelling
\item[* date of creation:] 22-JAN-1988 14:20:13  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 3/5/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added condition on particles (see below under `Presuppositions: rules').
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] spell out the reflexive of an inherently reflexive verb, in a 
form compatible with its subject antecedent, which is an NPVAR
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Animate NPs are supposed to have sexes (masculine, feminine or both). It is 
assumed that words like {\em baby\/} may not have [masculine,feminine] for 
sexes, but are split up. Otherwise, no use can be made of the disambiguating 
effect of the reflexive. If {\em baby\/} can also be referred to as {\em it\/},
it must occur in the dictionary for a third time, with sexes = [].\\
Inanimate 
NPs should have sexes = [], but may have gender specification (default = 
[omegagender]; words like {\em ship\/} may also have femininegender). In that 
case, they have a separate entry! (the set-value for the attribute `genders' is 
not used). This is needed to make full use of the disambiguating capacity of 
the reflexive. It is assumed 
that inanimates are always 3rd person, and that {\em one\/} is 3rd person sing.
\\ 
Since PERSPROs have gender i.s.o.\ sexes (a relict from Dutch), the NP covering 
a PERSPRO has an empty set for sexes, and the same gender(s) as the PERSPRO. 
Thus, for the reflexive spelling rules these persproNPs work the same as 
inanimate NPs with a gender (like {\em ship\/}).
\item rules:\\
It is assumed in this and the following three rules that inherently reflexive 
verbs may also have a particle (although no examples have been found yet). 
It is placed directly behind the verb, before the (object) reflexive.
This is the `standard' position when compared with particles occurring 
between verbs and real objects, as spelled out in TC\_ParticleSpelling. A later 
Particle Hop Rule obligatorily moves the particle over the reflexive 
(a reflexive variant of {\em He called himself up\/}).
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:] Quirk pp 187 - 192
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}


