\item date: 3/5/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Allow for particles in model (see what was said in TObjReflInsertion1 
under `Presuppositions: rules').
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] spell out the reflexive of an inherently reflexive verb, in a 
form compatible with its subject antecedent, which is a CNVAR
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule per 
form of the reflexive
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Animate CNs are supposed to have sexes (masculine, feminine or both) (see the 
remarks made in the previous rule under `presuppositions'); inanimate 
CNs should have sexes = [], but may have gender specification (default = 
omegagender; words like {\em ship\/} may also have femininegender). It is assumed 
that all CNs are 3rd person.\\
It is assumed that `optionally' reflexive verbs like {\em wash\/} and {\em 
adjust\/} are entered in the dictionary twice, once as an obligatorily 
reflexive verb (wash oneself), and once as an irrelexive (wash). The 
translation of the Dutch {\em zich wassen\/} will be to both these verbs. Note 
that {\em zichzelf wassen\/} is a case of an argument reflexive, which happens 
to translate into {\em wash oneself\/} too. Thus, the string {\em He washed 
himself\/} is ambiguous between {\em Hij waste zich\/} and {\em Hij waste 
zichzelf\/}, unless one of the two verbs is used in anlysis only.
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
