Allow for particles in model (see what was said in TObjReflInsertion1 
under `Presuppositions: rules').
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] spell out the indirect object reflexive of an inherently 
reflexive verb, in a 
form compatible with its subject antecedent, which is an NPVAR. It is assumed 
that indobj reflexives really exist (perhaps they just occur in idioms; or is {
\em He ordered himself a beer\/} an example? I don't know if {\em He ordered 
her a beer\/} is correct too; in that case it is just an argument reflexive, or 
some kind of benefactive, like {\em He bought her a house\/})
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule per 
form of the reflexive
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Animate NPs are supposed to have sexes (masculine or feminine, not both) (see 
the remarks made under TObjReflInsertion1); inanimate 
NPs and NPs on PERSPROs should have sexes = [], but may have gender 
specification (default = 
omegagender; words like {\em ship\/} may also have femininegender). It is assumed 
that inanimates are always 3rd person, and that {\em one\/} is 3rd person sing.
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
